{
 "title": "The GT-Score: A Robust Objective Function for Reducing Overfitting in Data-Driven Trading Strategies",
 "hypothesis": "Embedding performance, statistical significance (z-score), consistency (r2) and downside risk into the optimization objective (GT-Score) reduces overfitting and improves out-of-sample generalization vs Simple Profit, Sharpe and Sortino objectives.",
 "data_sources": [
  "yFinance API (yfinance Python package)",
  "Supplementary Materials GitHub: https://github.com/shep-analytics/gt_score"
 ],
 "sample": "Historical daily OHLCV for top 50 S&P 500 companies by market capitalization. Filters: sourced via yFinance; no survivor/liquidity filters stated beyond top 50 by market cap. Sample sizes: approximately 3770 trading days per asset from Jan 2010-Dec 2024; Monte Carlo study = 9000 optimization trials (50 assets x 4 loss functions x 3 strategies x 15 seeds); Walk-forward validation = 5340 sequential optimization trials (50 assets x 9 time splits x 4 loss functions with exclusions/invalids yielding 1335 per loss function as printed)",
 "period_start": "January 2010",
 "period_end": "December 2024",
 "method": [
  "1. Universe: top 50 S&P 500 by market capitalization; daily OHLCV via yFinance API; period Jan 2010 through Dec 2024; approx 3770 trading days per asset; Python 3.10 custom backtesting engine.",
  "2. Trading strategies with optimizable parameters (exact search ranges not stated): RSI (overbought/oversold thresholds); MACD (fast, slow, signal periods); Bollinger Bands (lookback window and band width).",
  "3. Walk-forward validation: Training window = 4 years, Validation window = 2 years, Step size = 1 year, Embargo = 30 days between train and validation. Generates 9 sequential train/validation splits per asset covering validation periods 2014-2024.",
  "4. Monte Carlo stability: 15 random seeds = 42-56 inclusive per configuration; Random search optimization = 25 parameter evaluations per trial; equal evaluation budget for all objectives. Total trials = 9000 Monte Carlo + 5340 walk-forward = over 14,000.",
  "5. Comparative objectives: Simple Loss = -Profit; Sharpe Ratio Loss = -Sharpe; Sortino Ratio Loss = -Sortino; GT-Score as objective to maximize (implemented as minimization of negative score).",
  "6. GT-Score calculation: GT_Score = mu * ln(z) * r2 / sigma_d. where z = (mu - mu_m) / (sigma / sqrt(N)). mu = mean strategy return per observation; mu_m = mean benchmark (buy-and-hold) per observation; sigma = std of strategy returns; N = number of return observations = number of executed trades in backtest window; r2 = R-squared consistency of returns; sigma_d = downside deviation. Epsilon = 1e-06 added to denominator to prevent division by zero.",
  "7. Piecewise handling (Algorithm 1): if z <=0: score = 100 + 100*(1 - exp(-|z-1|)) Large penalty; else if z <=1: score = 100*(1 - exp(-|z-1|)) Smooth transition; else (z>1): score = - mu*ln(z)*r2 / (sigma_d + epsilon) Standard GT-Score.",
  "8. Minimum sample size: n_min = 50 trades. Parameterizations generating <50 trades assigned large penalty during optimization. Fixed-threshold mode used for reported results. Optional stabilized variance periodization (not used for reported results): search for n* where variance of period returns changes by <1% across candidates, fallback n=50.",
  "9. Metrics: Test/Validation Mean return, Test/Validation Std, Train Mean return, Generalization Ratio = Test Return / Training Return or Validation Return / Training Return. Higher indicates better retention. Baseline Avg Gen Ratio = mean across Sharpe, Sortino, Simple.",
  "10. Statistical inference: Paired t-tests on Monte Carlo out-of-sample test returns (paired across trials) with mean difference, t-statistic, p-value, Cohen's d. Wilcoxon signed-rank paired non-parametric also reported as p-values only (statistic not printed). Emphasis on effect sizes and walk-forward results due to lack of explicit data-snooping correction.",
  "11. Regime and strategy breakdowns: Validation return by 9 walk-forward periods (2014-2016 through 2022-2024) reporting GT-Score, Baseline Avg and Delta in percentage points (GT minus Baseline). Mean out-of-sample return by strategy (RSI, MACD, Bollinger).",
  "12. Transaction-cost sensitivity: daily-bar backtest without execution modeling for main tables; sensitivity check subtracts additional per-side cost 0-10 bps per trade side (entry and exit) proportional to number of test-window trades. Mean test-window trades printed: GT-Score 32.4 vs ~21.0 for baselines."
 ],
